<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yuezhi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yuezhi were a nomadic people who lived in the region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Gansu corridor</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the late first millennium BCE. Defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> in the second century BCE, they migrated westwards to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, where one branch later founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a>. Accounts of the Xiongnu Empire discuss their defeat as part of the empire's rise and its wider impact.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yuezhi.jpg" alt="Yuezhi" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ALFGRN · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khalchayan_Prince,_side_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Yuezhi are first described in Chinese histories as nomadic pastoralists living in an arid grassland in the western part of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> during the 1st millennium BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzi">Guanzi</a> mentions nomads called the Yuzhi or Niuzhi who supplied jade to the Chinese and, unlike the neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, did not fight nearby Chinese states. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-han">Book of Han</a> says the Great Yuezhi were a nomadic horde with over a hundred thousand soldiers who lived between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> and Qilian. No archaeological remains of them have been found there, and some scholars place their homeland further northwest, north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a>. Many scholars believe they were an Indo-European people, though there is no evidence linking them to the Tarim mummies or the Tocharian languages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After a major defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> in 176 BCE the Yuezhi split into two groups. The Lesser Yuezhi moved to the southern mountains to live with the Qiang. The Greater Yuezhi began migrating north-west about 165 BCE and settled in the Ili valley, where they defeated the Sai. In 132 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wusun">Wusun</a>, allied with the Xiongnu, dislodged them, and they passed through Dayuan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Ferghana</a> and settled on the northern bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>.</p><p>A Chinese mission led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a> reached them in 126 BCE to seek an alliance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, but they refused, since they now lived peacefully and had no interest in revenge. Later Chinese accounts describe them as occupying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> in five major tribes, one of which, the Guishuang, took control of the confederation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Kushanas, widely believed to have been a dynastic clan or tribe of the Yuezhi, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a>. At its peak, in the 3rd century CE, it stretched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> on the Gangetic plain. The Kushans had an important part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> trade and in the introduction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The Yuezhi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> became Hellenized to some degree, adopting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-alphabet">Greek alphabet</a> and minting coins in Greek. The Lesser Yuezhi are linked to the Yicong Hu, who helped drive the Liangzhou Rebellion of 184-221 CE, and, controversially, to the Lushui Hu, who founded the Northern Liang in 397.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yuezhi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Yuezhi</b> who followed them adopted the Greek alphabet for their language and Greek gods for their pantheon, and the Bactrian language was written in Greek script until the 9th century CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 130 BCE the Central Asian recurve bow and its gorytos case appear on the coins of Zoilos I, a sign of contact and apparently alliance with the <b>Yuezhi</b> or the Scythians, and by 90 BCE it was standard for Indo-Greek horsemen, as Hermaeus's coins show.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kushans were one of five branches of the <b>Yuezhi</b>, a people driven from the Gansu corridor by the Xiongnu in the 2nd century BCE, who settled in Bactria, north of the Hindu Kush, by about 130 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Xiongnu defeated the <b>Yuezhi</b> in the Gansu corridor around 176 BCE, the Yuezhi moved west into the Ili valley and Zhetysu and drove out the Saka who lived there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Modu's campaigns united the peoples of Mongolia and Inner Asia, destroyed and absorbed the Donghu confederacy to the east, defeated the <b>Yuezhi</b> of the Gansu region, and brought peoples further west under Xiongnu suzerainty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Xiongnu turned the skull of a defeated <b>Yuezhi</b> king into a drinking cup; according to the Han shu, Han envoys later drank from it when swearing a blood oath with a Xiongnu chanyu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
